1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,450
You are 14, your family is starving, and you just lost your place at school.

2
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:10,900
Nobody in your village has power. What do you build?

3
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:16,950
This is the true story of William Kamkwamba, the boy who harnessed the wind.

4
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:23,400
And the part everyone remembers, the windmill, is not the most impressive thing he did.

5
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:35,850
By the end of this video you will know exactly how he built it, what he built it from, and why the hardest part had nothing to do with engineering.

6
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:41,500
Malawi, 2001. William lives in a farming village where almost nobody has electricity.

7
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,550
When the sun goes down, the day is over.

8
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:50,400
His family grows maize. That is their food and their income.

9
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:58,050
Then the rains fail. A drought, and then a famine. Families cut down to one meal a day.

10
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:04,500
William's father can no longer pay his school fees, so William has to leave school.

11
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:10,950
For most kids, that is the end of the story. Here is where William's begins.

12
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:18,200
He can't go to class, so he goes to the small library at his old primary school.

13
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:25,450
He doesn't have the English to read most of the books, so he reads the pictures. Diagrams.

14
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:30,700
And one book has a picture of a windmill on the cover.

15
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:37,550
The book is called Using Energy. And it explains something nobody around him had told him:

16
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:44,400
wind can turn a blade, the blade can turn a generator, and a generator makes electricity.

17
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:52,050
So William does the maths a hungry kid does. Electricity means light at night. It means a radio.

18
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:58,500
And if it can pump water, it means a second harvest in the dry season.

19
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,550
Which means the famine doesn't have to happen again.

20
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:07,400
But he has no money. So he goes to the scrapyard.

21
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:14,650
Here is the parts list, and every piece of it was something other people had thrown away.

22
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:19,500
A broken bicycle frame. A fan blade from an old tractor.

23
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,550
PVC pipe, which he heats and flattens into blades.

24
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:32,000
A small bicycle dynamo, the kind that powers a bike light. And for the tower, wood from blue gum trees.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:40,450
And this is the part I find hardest to imagine. Every day he walks to the dump. The village watches.

26
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:46,500
And they decide he has gone mad. The word they use is misala. Crazy.

27
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:50,150
He keeps going anyway. No teacher. No internet.

28
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:57,800
A book he can only half read, and parts he can't afford to replace if he breaks them.

29
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:05,050
Then he raises the tower, and the wind turns the blades, and a single bulb lights up.

30
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:09,900
Then a radio. In a house that has never had either.

31
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:15,150
The crowd that called him crazy turns up to charge their phones.

32
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:21,600
Here is the thing the film can't really show you. The windmill was the proof.

33
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:29,650
What William actually did was decide that being out of school was not the same as being finished learning.

34
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:37,700
Word gets out. A journalist writes about him. In 2007 he is invited to speak at TED, in Tanzania.

35
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:47,350
He has never been on a plane. He has never used a computer. He tells the room: I try, and I made it.

36
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:58,200
After that, there is a book, then a place at Dartmouth College in the United States, and in 2019 a Netflix film directed by Chiwetel Ejiofor.

37
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:04,650
And he went back. He built more windmills, a solar-powered pump for the village well,

38
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:10,700
and he has spent years helping other young people build things of their own.

39
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:16,350
So that is the story. A bicycle, a tractor fan, a library book,

40
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:22,800
and a kid who kept walking to the dump when everyone was laughing at him.

41
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:31,650
If you want the full version, read the book, The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind. It is linked in the description.
